# Memory Grid

Memory Grid starts from a simple premise: a memory is not safe just because it was once written.
The stage uses a small nonogram-like grid and a leaking boss to introduce maintenance, comparison,
and restoration.

The canonical tool is the diff viewer. The player receives two generated logs,
`memory_v1.log` and `memory_v2.log`. The first contains restoration chunks in changed sections.
The second has those chunks missing. Reading either file alone is not enough; the useful fact is the
difference between them.

In this first slice, the boss asks for the restoration key assembled from the changed hunks. Entering
the correct key sets `3.diff_key_restored`, unlocks the Memory Leak, fires the achievement, and makes
the grid solvable. The unlock does not defeat the boss. It only restores the missing column clues.

The full version of this stage can grow into the planned retention economy: fragments decay,
registers accumulate, and solved grids become memories that need active care. This slice keeps the
architecture focused while preserving the important contract for later work.
